    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `ReSTDirective.handle_signature`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `handle_signature` function in the `ReSTDirective` class processes the signature of a reStructuredText directive, breaking it into a name and arguments, and then constructs a representation of this signature within a Sphinx documentation node. It takes two parameters: `sig`, a string representing the directive's signature, and `signode`, a `desc_signature` node where the processed signature will be stored. The function first parses the signature into a name and arguments using the `parse_directive` helper function. It then sets the `fullname` attribute of the `signode` to the directive's name, adds a `desc_name` node with the directive's name prefixed by '.. ', and if there are any arguments, it adds a `desc_addname` node with the arguments. The function returns the directive's name. This function does not handle errors or edge cases explicitly; it assumes the input is well-formed. It has no side effects other than modifying the `signode` and returning the directive's name.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in